I think I agree with this. That is, I agree that DSM would still be
the best (unless other major changes were made), and I'm also happy
enough with DSM being the best. But like I said, I'd like to see the
other flavours of DSM getting a bit more play time. I'm still not sold
on the idea of mutually exclusive resistances. I'm trying some other
things.

I like the idea of each layer of armour contributing to MC. I think
that's probably how it should be. But for now I'm going to leave it
alone, because I've made a bunch of other changes that I want to test
out.

I'm getting a bit more comfortable with changing stuff, so my changes
are a bit more ambitious. Here's what I've changed recently:

I've made significant changes to the resistance system for poison,
fire, cold, and shock. In the code I now refer to 'immunity' and
'resistance' (although in-game both are still referred to as
resistance). 'Immunity' is pretty much the same as the vanilla
resistance, and 'resistance' roughly means that you'll takes half of
the normal damage =96 and I've written a bunch of new messages for this
new mid-level damage. External sources, such as rings of resistance
and DSM give 'immunity'; and intrinsic sources such as eating and
leveling up give 'resistance'. With intrinsic poison resistance, you
can no longer be instantly killed, but you can still receive damage
and have your stats lowered - but with reduced probably and magnitude.
The character's luck plays a role in some of the events where a player
can lose attribute points from poison; but the only way to completely
eliminate the possibility of attribute loss (from most sources) is
poison immunity. -- I haven't tested these resistance changes
thoroughly yet.

If you are polymorphed into a monster which has a particular natural
resistance, it will count as an immunity. For example, polymorphing
into a fire elemental will give you fire immunity rather than ordinary
resistance. Incidentally, the poison resistance that barbarians start
with at level 1 is currently seen by the code as a natural resistance,
and thus counts as poison immunity (as opposed to monks, which only
get poison resistance). I haven't decided if I should consider this to
be a bug or not...

While I was changing all this resistance stuff, I made a few other
minor changes. For example, magic resistance only blocks half the
damage from magic missile attacks; and I've changed a bunch of things
about how walking on and in lava works. There were a bunch of bugs in
the lava code, so I more or less rewrote it.

I've slowed down the speed of engraving. Specifically, engravings that
use to take (len/10) turns now take ((len+2)/5) turns. The point of
this change is to eliminate the possibility of instant-elbereth, but
without removing elbereth completely. Besides, I don't reckon anyone
could actually write Elbereth on the ground in the time it takes for
someone to walk through a door or swing a sword. This change is more
realistic!

I'm added the show-held-on-bottom-line patch and the msgtypes patch.

I've adjusted the bought protection formula _again_. I was unhappy
with the linear scaling. I think it was too cheap. Now the code is
something like this:
 if((rn2(15) - u.ublessed >=3D 0) && (rn2(15) - u.ublessed >=3D 0))
u.ublessed++;
So the maximum is 15, and the probability of getting each point
decreases smoothly, quadratically each time. With this formula, if you
spend 10 batches of gold then on average you'll get around 6 points of
protection. 20 batches will give around 9, and even 40 batches will
only give around 11. So effectively the maximum is actually less than
15. I'm pretty happy with this for now.

That's all for now.

I'm starting to be kind of happy with how things are shaping up. I
reckon I've improved the game. Maybe I should upload my code
somewhere...


